{"answers":[{"question_id":"Q021_nad_binding_convergent_evolution","answer":"SCOPe 2.08 illustrates that NAD(P) binding is not confined to one evolutionary lineage: examples include the NAD(P)-binding Rossmann fold c.2 (SUNID 51734; superfamily c.2.1, SUNID 51735), the FAD/NAD(P)-binding fold c.3 (SUNID 51904; superfamily c.3.1, SUNID 51905), the ferredoxin-like fold d.58 containing the NAD-binding domain of HMG-CoA reductase superfamily d.58.20 (SUNID 55035), and the DHS-like NAD/FAD-binding fold c.31 (SUNID 52466; superfamily c.31.1, SUNID 52467). SCOPe even comments that c.31.1 binds cofactors in the opposite direction from the classical Rossmann fold.

Within one SCOPe family, the classification supports a relatively close relationship; across families in one superfamily, it supports probable common ancestry and therefore a divergent-evolution interpretation, although it does not establish that NAD(P) binding itself was ancestral. Between different superfamilies—even within one fold—SCOPe makes no superfamily-level homology assertion. Occurrence in different folds is consistent with independent acquisition and convergent evolution toward solutions for binding the same cofactor, but classification separation alone does not prove convergence, independent origin, or absence of very remote homology.

Cofactor binding and active-site resemblance are local, functionally constrained features. Unrelated proteins can converge on glycine-rich loops, phosphate-binding residues, polar contacts, or similar catalytic geometry because NAD and NADP impose the same chemical and steric requirements. Such resemblance therefore cannot substitute for evidence of correspondence across the domain core. A stronger homology or convergence conclusion requires global structural alignment and topology, statistically meaningful sequence correspondence at equivalent core positions, motifs compared in equivalent structural contexts, compatible domain architecture and biological context, and phylogenetic or literature evidence. Ligand-bound structures plus biochemical, kinetic, and mutational evidence are additionally needed to transfer NAD-versus-NADP preference, substrate specificity, reaction chemistry, catalytic mechanism, or active-site function; neither shared cofactor binding nor even a shared superfamily safely establishes those properties.","evidence_used":["scop-homology/SKILL.md","scop-homology/references/biological-interpretation.md","scop-homology/references/api-usage.md","/api/v1/releases/current","/api/v1/releases/2.08/search?q=NAD%28P%29-binding&limit=10","/api/v1/releases/2.08/search?q=NAD-binding&limit=20","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/51735/parents","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/51905/parents","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/55035/parents","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/52467/parents","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/51735/annotations","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/51735/homology","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/51905/annotations","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/51905/homology","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/55035/annotations","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/55035/homology","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/52467/annotations","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/52467/homology"]}]}
